        <encodingDesc>
            <projectDesc>
               <p>The Welte-Mignon Advertisements Collection brings together print
                  advertisements for the Welte-Mignon reproducing piano that appeared
                  between 1905 and 1930. It was created as part of the PhD project
                  "Grünfelds Geist" at the University of Tübingen, which considers
                  piano rolls as objects of a digital edition. The collection is
                  published at https://anz.welte225.org.</p>
            </projectDesc>
            <samplingDecl>
               <p>The collection gathers print advertisements for the Welte-Mignon
                  reproducing piano that appeared between 1905 and 1930 and were
                  published by M. Welte &amp; Söhne, by one of its licensees, or by a
                  dealer or agent selling the instrument. Each advertisement is
                  encoded once; its further printings, in the same or in other
                  periodicals, are listed as further sources.</p>
               <p>The advertisements were found in digitised newspapers and
                  magazines: Chronicling America (Library of Congress), the British
                  Newspaper Archive, HathiTrust (copies of the Universities of
                  California, Illinois, Michigan, Minnesota and Virginia), the
                  Internet Archive, the Digitale Sammlungen of the Bayerische
                  Staatsbibliothek, ANNO (Austrian National Library), the
                  International Arcade Museum Library, Georgia Historic Newspapers,
                  Delpher (KB, National Library of the Netherlands),
                  eluxemburgensia (National Library of Luxembourg), RetroNews
                  (Bibliothèque nationale de France), the archives of Le Temps,
                  the Nasjonalbiblioteket of Norway, Mediestream (Royal Danish
                  Library), the National Library of Latvia (through Europeana),
                  Canadiana, the Hemeroteca Nacional Digital de México, the
                  St Petersburg State Theatre Library, Trove (National Library of
                  Australia), the newspapers of the Kungliga biblioteket of Sweden,
                  e-newspaperarchives.ch, BAnQ numérique (Bibliothèque et Archives
                  nationales du Québec), the Biblioteca Virtual de Prensa
                  Histórica, the archive of ABC, ARCA (Biblioteca de Catalunya)
                  and Polona (National Library of Poland).</p>
            </samplingDecl>
            <editorialDecl>
               <p>Each file transcribes one printing of an advertisement, the one
                  whose scan its facsimile zones refer to; <att>decls</att> on
                  <gi>text</gi> points to its <gi>sourceDesc</gi>. The further
                  printings were identified as the same advertisement but not
                  collated word for word; differences that were noticed are
                  recorded with <gi>app</gi>, whose first <gi>rdg</gi> is that of
                  the transcribed printing, or in a note. A printing that was set
                  anew counts as the same advertisement as long as its text
                  differs only in spelling, punctuation, a few words or a
                  sentence; its note describes the setting, whose line breaks and
                  type are not recorded.</p>
               <p>The regions and lines of the scans were mostly marked in
                  eScriptorium and converted from PAGE XML to TEI. The text was in
                  part recognised automatically (HTR) and corrected against the
                  scan, in part transcribed by hand. The advertisements published
                  outside Germany, the United Kingdom and the United States, and a
                  few from Germany, were transcribed from the scans, encoded, given
                  their regions by Claude, an AI model; their revision history says
                  so. Until the editor has checked such an advertisement against the
                  scan, <att>status</att> on its <gi>revisionDesc</gi> is
                  <val>unchecked</val>; that of every other advertisement is
                  <val>checked</val>. The topics of all advertisements were assigned
                  by Claude.</p>
               <correction>
                  <p>Errors of the original are not corrected but marked with
                     <gi>sic</gi>.</p>
               </correction>
               <normalization>
                  <p>Spelling and punctuation follow the original and are not
                     normalised. Text set in capitals is often transcribed in mixed
                     case and marked with the rendition <val>#uc</val>.</p>
               </normalization>
               <hyphenation eol="all">
                  <p>Hyphens at the end of a line are kept as printed.</p>
               </hyphenation>
               <segmentation>
                  <p>Every region of the original page is a <gi>div</gi> that points to
                     its zone in the <gi>facsimile</gi> with <att>facs</att>. The
                     lines of the original are marked with <gi>lb</gi>; the typography
                     is recorded with <att>rendition</att>. The zones refer to the
                     first <gi>graphic</gi> of a <gi>surface</gi>, the scan they were
                     drawn on. Where the holding library serves the page through
                     IIIF, a second <gi>graphic</gi> of the type <val>IIIF</val>
                     gives that image and its size.</p>
               </segmentation>
               <interpretation>
                  <p>Parts of an advertisement that concern other products than the
                     Welte-Mignon are not transcribed but marked with <gi>gap</gi>,
                     which describes them in a <gi>desc</gi>. Illustrations are
                     described in <gi>figure</gi>. Persons are linked to the GND with
                     <att>corresp</att>; firms are linked to the GND or, where it
                     has no record of them, to Wikidata. Recurring arguments and motifs are marked as
                     topics with <att>ana</att> on <gi>seg</gi> and <gi>figure</gi>,
                     pointing to the taxonomy of topics. The taxonomy was induced by
                     Claude in four blind readings of all advertisements, each in a
                     different order and without knowledge of the editor's earlier
                     taxonomy, which it replaced in October 2026; it keeps the topics
                     that at least three of the readings found. The topics are an
                     interpretation, assigned by whom <att>resp</att> says, so far
                     all by Claude; the editor has not yet checked them.</p>
               </interpretation>
               <stdVals>
                  <p>Dates of publication are given in ISO 8601 in <att>when</att>,
                     as precisely as they are known.</p>
               </stdVals>
            </editorialDecl>
            <schemaRef type="projectODD" url="schema/welte-ads.odd"/>
            <tagsDecl>
                <rendition xml:id="c" scheme="css">text-align: center;</rendition>
            </tagsDecl>
            <classDecl>
                <taxonomy xml:id="topics">
                    <desc>Topics: the recurring arguments and motifs of the advertisements,
                       in their texts and in their pictures</desc>
                    <category xml:id="faithful_reproduction">
                       <gloss>Faithful reproduction</gloss>
                       <desc>The ad claims that the playing of the artist is reproduced exactly,
                          faithfully, naturally or completely, as "the actual playing" of the
                          pianist. A bare "reproduces the playing of …" without such a
                          qualifier does not count.</desc>
                    </category>
                    <category xml:id="touch_and_individuality">
                       <gloss>Touch, nuance and individuality</gloss>
                       <desc>The ad names what is kept beyond the notes: touch, phrasing,
                          pedalling, dynamics, tempo, shading, tone colour, or the
                          individuality and characteristic style of each pianist.</desc>
                    </category>
                    <category xml:id="the_artists_soul">
                       <gloss>The artist's soul</gloss>
                       <desc>The instrument is said to capture the soul, spirit, feeling or
                          inner life of the performer, a spiritual register beyond the
                          technical one of touch and nuance ("the living soul of the artist",
                          "Musikseele").</desc>
                    </category>
                    <category xml:id="illusion_of_presence">
                       <gloss>Illusion of presence</gloss>
                       <desc>The listener feels that the artist is present or seated at the
                          instrument ("as if he sat at your piano", "close your eyes"), or
                          cannot tell the reproduction from the live artist.</desc>
                    </category>
                    <category xml:id="artists_hear_themselves">
                       <gloss>Artists hear themselves</gloss>
                       <desc>The artists vouch for their own recordings: they hear or recognise
                          their own playing, are deceived by it, sign the rolls as
                          autographs, or a demonstration proves that each pianist can be
                          recognised.</desc>
                    </category>
                    <category xml:id="immortality">
                       <gloss>Immortality and posterity</gloss>
                       <desc>The playing is preserved for ever or for future generations; the
                          artists become immortal, and dead artists still play.</desc>
                    </category>
                    <category xml:id="wonder">
                       <gloss>Wonder, miracle and magic</gloss>
                       <desc>The instrument is called a wonder, miracle, marvel or magic, or it
                          causes astonishment, disbelief or speechlessness.</desc>
                    </category>
                    <category xml:id="phantom_hands">
                       <gloss>Phantom hands</gloss>
                       <desc>The absent performer appears as a phantom or spirit: ghostly or
                          semi-transparent hands and figures, keys moving with no one seated,
                          the artist summoned at will ("zitieren", "summon").</desc>
                    </category>
                    <category xml:id="masters_at_home">
                       <gloss>The great pianists in your home</gloss>
                       <desc>The famous pianists can be heard in your own home or on your own
                          piano, at any time, as often as you wish, at your command ("The
                          Master's Fingers on Your Piano", concerts at home).</desc>
                    </category>
                    <category xml:id="refinement">
                       <gloss>Culture, refinement and distinction</gloss>
                       <desc>Owning the instrument marks culture, refinement or social standing
                          ("vornehmes Heim", "a visible token of culture"), or the picture
                          places it in an elegant upper-class setting.</desc>
                    </category>
                    <category xml:id="testimonials">
                       <gloss>Testimonials of famous musicians</gloss>
                       <desc>A named musician or other notable person is quoted or paraphrased
                          in praise of the instrument.</desc>
                    </category>
                    <category xml:id="epoch_making_invention">
                       <gloss>Epoch-making invention</gloss>
                       <desc>The instrument is framed as an invention: epoch-making, ingenious,
                          the greatest of the age, a sensation, the newest thing.</desc>
                    </category>
                    <category xml:id="superlatives">
                       <gloss>Superlative perfection</gloss>
                       <desc>The instrument is called perfect, the best, the finest, supreme or
                          unsurpassed, or the culmination of a line of progress.</desc>
                    </category>
                    <category xml:id="the_original">
                       <gloss>The original</gloss>
                       <desc>The Welte-Mignon is the original or the first reproducing piano,
                          protected by the original patents; the history of the Welte firm
                          and its founders guarantees it.</desc>
                    </category>
                    <category xml:id="education">
                       <gloss>Education and study</gloss>
                       <desc>The instrument serves learning and teaching: conservatories,
                          students, studying an interpretation by repeating it.</desc>
                    </category>
                    <category xml:id="still_a_piano">
                       <gloss>Still a normal piano</gloss>
                       <desc>The instrument remains an ordinary piano: it can be played by hand,
                          the mechanism is invisible and leaves its look unchanged, it can be
                          fitted to many makes of piano.</desc>
                    </category>
                </taxonomy>
            </classDecl>
